    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Hi, I'm looking for some clarification on UK inheritance tax (IHT) regarding assets held in offshore trusts. If a deceased individual was a UK resident but held significant real estate in a non-resident trust, how is the valuation of those specific assets treated for the purpose of calculating the taxable estate? Also, are there any common pitfalls where assets might be overlooked during the initial filing?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "For a deceased UK resident, the real estate held within a non-resident trust is generally considered part of their estate for Inheritance Tax (IHT) purposes, even if the trust itself is offshore. The valuation of these assets is typically taken at market value at the time of death, just like any other asset in the deceased's estate. A common pitfall is failing to correctly identify the beneficial interests or the extent to which the deceased retained control or entitlement over those assets, which can lead to the tax authority treating them as part of the estate. It's crucial to trace the connection between the deceased and the trust structure.",
